---
/* HeldScene — the one scene on this site that stops.

   WHY IT EXISTS. refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md measured the reference and found
   that its DOM is inert: every large element holds transform:none, opacity:1
   and clip-path:none across a scroll, and all of its motion lives in one
   fixed WebGL canvas the brief forbids us. But the GRAMMAR under that canvas
   costs nothing and is not WebGL. The subject is held in place for about two
   and a half viewport heights while the environment changes around it and
   successive text beats fade through. Hold the visual, move the type.

   Everywhere else this site does the inverse — the type holds its column and
   the photographs scroll past it — which is why it reads as a well-set
   document rather than as a sequence of scenes.

   THE THREE THINGS IT DOES AT ONCE. It is not a hold unless it does all
   three; a picture that merely pins is a stuck page.

     1. THE PICTURE HOLDS.  A `position: sticky` stage one viewport high
        inside a tall track. Native scroll: nothing is hijacked and nothing
        is trapped, the wheel does what it does everywhere else on the page,
        and the scrollbar never lies about where the reader is.

     2. THE PICTURE IS NEVER STATIC.  A slow scroll-linked scale across the
        whole hold — the 2D equivalent of the reference's camera push. It is
        continuous and reversible, like `data-settle`; it is not an entrance.

     3. THE GROUND MOVES.  This is what makes the hold an event rather than a
        pause. The photograph starts at page ground, opens out of it and
        closes back into it (`arc="lift"`, the default); or falls away to
        isolate the subject (`arc="fall"`, the reference's literal move); or
        opens and stays open to hand a lit picture to what follows
        (`arc="rise"`). Measured at the page edges that is a swing of about
        thirty L* inside one scene. The homepage before this component held a
        5.7-point band across 79% of its scroll.

   ONE PER SITE. A site where every section pins is a gimmick, and gimmick is
   a tone failure on a civic institute's site. If a second scene wants this,
   one of the two is not the scene the page is about.

   ── invocation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     <HeldScene
       src={forum.image} alt=""
       eyebrow="The Lion Forum"
       credit={forum.credit}
       focal="52% 48%"
       beats={[
         'Founders, investors and officials, in one room.',
         { line: 'One weekend a year.', note: 'Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port' },
       ]}>
       <a slot="coda" class="label label--lit" href="/forum/">How the Forum works</a>
     </HeldScene>

   Copy arrives as data, per AGENTS.md — never hard-code it here or in a
   page. A beat is ONE short line. Three is the ceiling and two is usually
   right: the hold is long, and a reader who has been given four things to
   read has been given a page, not a scene. A beat that needs a second
   sentence is a scene of its own.

   ── degradation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   The pinned layout is opt-in twice over: it needs `html.js` AND
   `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`. The DEFAULT rendering — what a
   reduced-motion reader and a no-JS reader both get — is a plain stacked
   figure with the beats set below it on the page ground: no sticky and no
   overlap. That is a real composition, not a fallback that merely avoids
   crashing.

   IT IS NOT, HOWEVER, "no type on photography at all" — this comment said
   that for two waves and it was never true. `.held__fig .fig__credit` is set
   ON the picture in this layout too, which is why `.held__cap` is drawn here
   (see its rule below). Do not restore the "no type on photography" claim.

   AND THE CAP WAS NOT UNDER IT. The wave-15 judge measured that credit at
   1.13:1 desktop / 1.21:1 mobile under `reduce` and concluded "the cap is not
   buying enough". The cap was buying nothing, because it was on the WRONG
   SIDE of the glyphs: this scene's whole ink stack was a SIBLING of the
   figure, and `.fig` sets `isolation: isolate`, so no z-index out here can
   reach beneath a layer in there. Differenced glyph-against-backdrop in all
   three render modes, the ground under that credit read 0.006 relative
   luminance — already deep — while the cream ink read 0.039-0.079 against the
   0.60 the same string reads on /forum/. The cap was painting the DISCLAIMER
   out, and every previous wave's instinct (more cap, heavier cap) made it
   worse. `.held__grade` is now handed to Figure's `grade` slot, which renders
   it above the grain and below the credit; measured after, the same credit
   reads 7.67-11.80:1 across the six mode x viewport combinations. Nothing
   about the ink's weight, height or position changed — only its paint order.

   The reason this matters more than a contrast budget: refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md
   puts that credit there to stop a Block Island harbour reading as the
   Kennedy Compound named three lines below it. A credit nobody can read is a
   disclaimer that is not doing its job.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import Figure from './Figure.astro';

const {
  src,
  alt = '',
  /* How many viewport heights the picture is held for after it pins. 2.5 is
     the reference's own figure. Below ~1.6 the hold reads as a stutter; above
     ~3 the reader starts checking the scrollbar. */
  hold = 2.5,
  holdSm = 1.7,
  /* THE CAMERA PUSH: scale added across the whole hold. Small on purpose —
     this is weight, not a zoom. Above ~0.10 it reads as a transition rather
     than as a room you are standing in. */
  push = 0.075,
  arc = 'lift',            // 'lift' | 'fall' | 'rise'
  /* How far out of the ground the picture comes at the top of the arc.
     1 = the house grade and nothing on top of it. */
  open = 1,
  /* How deep the ground is at the closed end of the arc. 1 = page ground —
     and 1 is a trap. At 0.93 the arc closed onto a mean sRGB of 40 across the
     middle half of the viewport, against a page ground of 13 and a plateau of
     142: numerically distinct from navy, visually not. The wave-11 blind read
     lost MOTION on that exit frame — a full 900px viewport holding one 12px
     link and a picture too dark to read — and lost the entry frame beside it.
     0.72 lands the two ends on 67. The arc still swings 75 points inside one
     scene, which is the event; what changed is that both ends of it are now a
     photograph you can read rather than a rectangle of ground. The eyebrow is
     unaffected either way: its worst ground is mid-plateau, where --open is 1
     and this number contributes nothing (measured 4.58:1 at 390px both before
     and after — the long-quoted 4.70 was a 16-sample reading of the same
     sweep; at 40 samples the floor of that curve is 4.58). */
  floor = 0.72,
  /* Lower than the house 0.5 because the arc supplies the rest of the ink,
     and supplies it as an event instead of as a constant. */
  grade = 0.34,
  vig = 0.55,
  focal = '50% 50%',
  focalSm,
  zoom = 1,
  zoomSm,
  eyebrow,                 // names the scene; held for the whole pin
  /* A held scene that REPLACES a titled section has to keep that section's
     heading, or the page loses a rung in its outline. Pass the id the
     section's aria-labelledby points at and the FIRST beat's line is set as
     the <h2> instead of a <p>. Nothing about its size or position changes —
     a beat is a beat; this is semantics, not a second display rung. */
  headingId,
  /* THE METER — one short hairline per beat, filled in brass as its beat
     arrives and NOT emptied afterwards, so the meter reads how far through
     the scene the reader is rather than which beat happens to be lit. It was
     wired to the beat's own envelope until wave 21, which meant it read zero
     at the seam between two beats — a duration mark answering nothing in the
     middle of the duration. See THE METER LATCHES in motion.js.
     Defaults on at 2+ beats. It is deliberately NOT a numeral: a counter set
     over a photograph is either bright enough to compete with the eyebrow or
     dim enough to fail the legibility budget, and either way it is a fourth
     mark in a scene whose whole argument is that there is one picture and one
     line. A rule is a mark this site already owns (.cue, .forum__rule), it
     carries no words, and it answers the only question a held scene actually
     raises: how long is this. */
  counter,
  beats = [],              // string | { line, note }
  /* THE NOTE'S REGISTER. A beat's note defaults to the micro `.label` voice,
     which is right for a duration or a count. It is WRONG for a place-name:
     tracked micro-caps at low contrast over a full-bleed photograph is
     exactly the shape of a photo credit, and a scene whose picture was taken
     somewhere other than the place it names cannot afford that reading. Pass
     `noteAs="bridge"` and the note is set at the bridge rung instead — plain
     sentence case, sans, one rung under the display line, which is the voice
     the site uses for "this is a fact about the subject" rather than "this is
     a fact about the photograph". See refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md. */
  noteAs = 'label',
  credit,
  /* BOTTOM, not top. Figure's own default puts the location credit under the
     bar — which is invisible below the fold, because Nav.astro's scrim is
     opaque from the first pixel of scroll, and unprotected the moment you
     move it clear of that scrim. On a held scene the picture is open for two
     viewport heights and the credit has to survive it, so it rides the foot
     ink instead, in the corner opposite the beats. */
  creditPos = 'bottom-right',
  /* THE APPROACH. A held scene is preceded by whatever section precedes it,
     and that section ends on its own `--scene-pad`. Between a text section and
     another text section that padding is the rest a reader needs. Between a
     text section and a FULL-BLEED PHOTOGRAPH it is a band of bare ground with
     nothing in it — measured on the homepage at 1440, 219px from the last
     pilot rule to the picture's top edge, then ~77px more before the sky is
     legible: 296px, a third of a viewport, crossed with no signal that
     anything is coming. That was the wave-11 PACING loss, and it is present
     under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` too, so it is the static render.
     `pull` takes a share of that padding back, in the padding's own units so
     it scales the way the page does. It is spacing, not composition: nothing
     inside the scene moves relative to anything else. */
  pull = 0,                // multiple of --scene-pad to close before the scene
  ratio = '16 / 9',        // the shape of the STACKED (reduced-motion) figure
  ratioSm = '4 / 5',
  align = 'left',          // which wall the beats are set against
  class: klass = '',
} = Astro.props;

const items = beats.map((b) => (typeof b === 'string' ? { line: b } : b));
const showCount = counter === undefined ? items.length > 1 : !!counter;
const hasCoda = Astro.slots.has('coda');

const style = [
  `--hold:${hold}`,
  `--hold-sm:${holdSm}`,
  `--push:${push}`,
  `--open-max:${open}`,
  `--floor:${floor}`,
  `--held-ratio:${ratio}`,
  `--held-ratio-sm:${ratioSm}`,
  `--held-origin:${focal}`,
  `--pull:${pull}`,
].join(';');
---
<section
  class:list={['held', { 'held--right': align === 'right' }, klass]}
  style={style}
  aria-labelledby={headingId}
  data-hold
  data-arc={arc}
>
  <div class="held__stage" data-hold-stage>
    <Figure src={src} alt={alt} ratio="auto"
            grade={grade} vig={vig} scrim="none"
            focal={focal} focalSm={focalSm} zoom={zoom} zoomSm={zoomSm}
            credit={credit} creditPos={creditPos}
            loading="lazy" sizes="100vw" class="held__fig">

    {/* THE GRADE STACK. One box that always covers exactly the photograph
        and nothing else — inset:0 over the pinned stage, and the stacked
        figure's own aspect box when there is no pin. Everything that inks
        the picture lives inside it, so nothing can drift onto the beats when
        the layout changes underneath it.

        ITS OWN BACKGROUND IS THE ARC: one navy sheet whose weight is the
        inverse of --open, radial rather than flat, so the frame's edges
        carry more ink than its middle and the environment closes in on the
        subject instead of the whole picture dimming. This is the tonal
        event. The photograph under it is never touched — the house grade
        stays the house grade. */}
    <div class="held__grade" slot="grade" aria-hidden="true">
      {/* THE BAR BAND, CONTINUED. Figure guarantees ink under the fixed nav
          on every full-bleed frame, but it guarantees exactly the bar's
          height and releases hard — invisible under the house grade of 0.5,
          and a drawn line across the sky at the 0.34 this component uses.
          This carries the release further down, and it opens with the arc,
          so it is absent at the closed end where nothing needs protecting.
          It is also what the eyebrow is set on. */}
      <div class="held__cap"></div>

      {/* PROTECTION, AND IT TRACKS THE ARC. The beats sit in one band at the
          foot of the frame and never leave it, so the ink that carries them
          is placed rather than spread. But the picture under that band gets
          brighter as the arc opens, so the ink has to open with it: a fixed
          ramp tuned at the closed end fails at the top of the arc, which is
          exactly the failure tools/photo-meter.mjs exists to catch. */}
      <div class="held__ink"></div>

      {/* THE FOOT. A short full-width band at the bottom edge — the house
          seam, and what carries the corner credit. Separate from the beat
          ink because that ink is masked to the type's own column and hands
          the rest of the frame back to the photograph. */}
      <div class="held__foot"></div>
    </div>
    </Figure>

    {(eyebrow || showCount) && (
      <div class="shell held__top">
        {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow held__brow">{eyebrow}</p>}
        {showCount && (
          <p class="held__meter" aria-hidden="true">
            {items.map((_, i) => <i class="held__tick" data-beat-tick={i}></i>)}
          </p>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    <div class="shell held__beats">
      {items.map((b, i) => (
        <div class="held__beat" data-beat={i}>
          {headingId && i === 0
            ? <h2 class="display display--head held__line" id={headingId}>{b.line}</h2>
            : <p class="display display--head held__line">{b.line}</p>}
          {b.note && <p class:list={[noteAs, 'held__note', noteAs === 'bridge' && 'held__note--bridge']}>{b.note}</p>}
        </div>
      ))}
      {hasCoda && <div class="held__coda" data-coda><slot name="coda" /></div>}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ── DEFAULT: the stacked layout ──────────────────────────────────
     No sticky, no absolute positioning, no type over photography. This is
     what a reduced-motion reader and a no-JS reader get, and it has to be a
     composition in its own right rather than a wreck that happens to be
     readable. */
  .held {
    position: relative;
    --open: 1;                 /* the engine overwrites; 1 keeps it open */
    --hp: 0;
    /* Static, so it costs no CLS and it is identical with JS off and with
       motion off — which is where the gap was measured. */
    margin-top: calc(var(--scene-pad) * var(--pull, 0) * -1);
  }
  /* The meter counts beats through a hold. With no hold it is a mark that
     measures nothing, so it is not drawn. */
  .held__meter { display: none; }
  .held__ink { display: none; }
  /* THE CAP, IN THE STACKED LAYOUT TOO. It used to be display:none here, on
     the reasoning that the stacked figure carries no type. It carries one
     piece: the photograph's own location credit, which `creditPos="top-*"`
     puts on the sky. Measured on the reduced-motion homepage that credit read
     1.68 : 1 against a 4.5 budget — and neither photo-meter nor hold-meter
     could see it, because both run with motion enabled and the enhanced cap
     covers it there. So the cap is drawn here as well, sized to the figure
     instead of to the viewport, and at a fixed weight: there is no arc in
     this layout, so there is nothing for it to track.

     READ THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE BEFORE TOUCHING THE WEIGHTS. Between that wave
     and this one, this cap did not protect the credit — it painted over it,
     because the whole grade stack sat outside the figure's stacking context.
     It is inside it now. If a credit here ever measures thin again, check
     paint order before you reach for opacity: this ink darkens the cream and
     the picture in exactly the same proportion, so adding weight cannot
     move the ratio. */
  .held__cap {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0; top: 0; height: 36%;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg,
      rgb(3 9 16 / 0.68) 0%,
      rgb(3 9 16 / 0.56) 44%,
      rgb(3 9 16 / 0.28) 74%,
      transparent 100%);
  }

  .held__stage { position: relative; isolation: isolate; }

  /* THE GRADE BOX, stacked: exactly the figure's aspect box, at the top of
     the stage. The stacked figure carries no type, so it needs no
     protection — but it still has to belong to the same commission as every
     other photograph on the site. `grade` is set low on the figure because
     the arc supplies the rest of the ink, and with no arc the picture would
     land two stops brighter than the rest of the site: one flat sheet puts
     it back on the house grade, and the foot gives the bottom edge the same
     seam every other full-bleed frame has. */
  /* INSIDE THE FIGURE, VIA ITS `grade` SLOT — and that is the fix, not a
     tidy-up. This box used to be a SIBLING of the figure at z-index 1, which
     put every layer in it (the arc sheet, the cap, the foot) ON TOP OF the
     photograph's location credit: `.fig` sets `isolation: isolate`, so a
     z-index out here can never reach beneath a layer in there. The credit
     measured 1.79-2.28 : 1 in all three render modes with a ground of 0.006 —
     the ink under the credit was already deep, the cream glyphs were the
     thing being painted out. Figure renders the slot above its grain and
     below its credit, so the same ink now lands where it was always meant to.
     It is also exactly the figure's own box in both layouts, which is what
     the duplicated aspect-ratio was reconstructing by hand. */
  .held__grade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: rgb(5 13 22 / 0.14);
  }
  .held__foot {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; height: 15%;
    background: linear-gradient(to top,
      var(--ink-900) 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 52%, transparent) 34%,
      transparent 100%);
  }

  .held :global(.held__fig) {
    position: relative;
    inset: auto;
    z-index: 0;
    aspect-ratio: var(--held-ratio);
  }

  /* Eyebrow over meter, both against the left rail. They are NOT split to
     the two corners: the figure's own location credit owns a top corner on
     every full-bleed frame on this site, and a mark set opposite an eyebrow
     lands exactly on top of it. */
  .held__top {
    width: 100%;
    display: grid;
    justify-items: start;
    gap: clamp(0.6rem, 1.4vh, 0.95rem);
    padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem) 0;
  }
  .held__meter { gap: 0.5rem; }
  .held__tick {
    display: block;
    width: clamp(14px, 2.2vw, 26px);
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--mark);
    position: relative;
  }
  /* the live beat's tick, drawn over the dim one so nothing reflows */
  .held__tick::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute; inset: 0;
    background: var(--brass);
    transform: scaleX(var(--be, 0));
    transform-origin: left;
  }
  .held__tick:first-child::after { transform: scaleX(var(--be, 1)); }

  .held__beats {
    width: 100%;
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(1.6rem, 3.4vw, 2.8rem);
    padding-block: clamp(1.6rem, 3.4vw, 2.6rem) clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .held__line { max-width: 18ch; }
  .held__note { margin-top: clamp(0.7rem, 1.5vw, 1.05rem); }
  /* The bridge rung sits directly under the display line it belongs to, so
     it reads as part of the same stack rather than as a separate mark. */
  .held__note--bridge { margin-top: clamp(0.35rem, 0.8vw, 0.6rem); color: var(--fg-mute); max-width: 26ch; }
  .held--right .held__note--bridge { margin-left: auto; }

  .held--right .held__line, .held--right .held__note { margin-left: auto; text-align: right; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .held :global(.held__fig) { aspect-ratio: var(--held-ratio-sm); }
    .held__line { max-width: 14ch; }
    /* The small-screen crop is a 4/5 portrait, so the credit sits higher up a
       brighter sky than it does on the 16/9: measured 4.28 : 1 at the desktop
       weight, against 4.5. Taller and heavier here, for the same reason the
       beat ink is. */
    .held__cap {
      height: 42%;
      background: linear-gradient(180deg,
        rgb(3 9 16 / 0.80) 0%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / 0.68) 48%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / 0.34) 78%,
        transparent 100%);
    }
  }

  /* ── ENHANCED: the hold ───────────────────────────────────────────
     Gated on BOTH `html.js` and a reader who has not asked for less motion.
     Everything here is position and paint. The engine reads one rect per
     frame and writes custom properties; it never touches layout. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* the track: one viewport for the stage, plus the held length.
       svh, not dvh — a track whose height changed when a phone's address bar
       retracted would move the page under the reader's thumb. */
    :global(html.js) .held { height: calc((1 + var(--hold)) * 100svh); }
    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      :global(html.js) .held { height: calc((1 + var(--hold-sm)) * 100svh); }
    }

    :global(html.js) .held__stage {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      /* dvh HERE, though: the stage is what the reader looks at, and a
         100svh stage leaves a strip of page ground under a full-bleed
         picture the moment a phone's address bar goes away. `overflow: clip`
         does not create a scroll container, so it cannot break the sticky. */
      height: 100svh;
      height: 100dvh;
      isolation: isolate;
      overflow: clip;
    }

    :global(html.js) .held :global(.held__fig) {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }
    :global(html.js) .held__grade { inset: 0; }
    /* THE PUSH — continuous, scroll-linked, reversible, taken around the same
       point the crop is composed on, so the frame opens into its own subject
       rather than into its middle.

       It is on the MEDIA layer, not on the figure. Scaling the whole figure
       scales its treatment with it: the bar band drifts up off the bar, the
       vignette grows past the corners it was drawn for, and the location
       credit walks up behind the fixed nav. Protection that moves is
       protection that stops covering the thing it was placed over.
       (`parallax` must stay 0 on a held figure — it writes --py into this
       same transform, and the two would fight.) */
    :global(html.js) .held :global(.held__fig .fig__media) {
      transform: scale(calc(1 + var(--push) * var(--hp)));
      transform-origin: var(--held-origin);
      will-change: transform;
    }

    :global(html.js) .held__grade {
      --a: calc(var(--floor) * (1 - var(--open) * var(--open-max)));
      background: radial-gradient(126% 96% at 50% 46%,
        rgb(5 13 22 / calc(var(--a) * 0.82)) 0%,
        rgb(5 13 22 / calc(var(--a) * 0.93)) 50%,
        rgb(5 13 22 / calc(var(--a) * 1.10 + 0.05)) 100%);
    }

    :global(html.js) .held__cap {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      left: 0; right: 0; top: 0;
      pointer-events: none;
      height: calc(var(--nav-h) + 5.5rem + 16vh);
      background: linear-gradient(180deg,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(0.46 * var(--open))) 0%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(0.42 * var(--open))) 58%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(0.24 * var(--open))) 80%,
        transparent 100%);
    }

    :global(html.js) .held__foot {
      height: 15%;
      /* A PLATEAU, not a wedge — the same lesson Figure's own bottom ramp
         records. The credit sits about 4% up from the frame's edge, so ink
         that peaks at the edge and is already spent by the time it reaches
         the words protects the padding and not the type. Measured: the wedge
         left the credit on L* 23 and 4.2:1 against a 4.5 budget. */
      background: linear-gradient(0deg,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(0.42 + 0.54 * var(--open))) 0%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc((0.42 + 0.54 * var(--open)) * 0.94)) 26%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc((0.42 + 0.54 * var(--open)) * 0.62)) 52%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc((0.42 + 0.54 * var(--open)) * 0.20)) 76%,
        transparent 100%);
    }

    :global(html.js) .held__ink {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
      height: 58%;
      /* THE BASE TERM IS THE FLOOR'S OTHER HALF. This ramp only has to carry
         the difference between the picture and the type; at the CLOSED end of
         the arc the grade used to carry most of it, so 0.26 was enough. When
         `floor` came down from 0.93 to 0.72 — so that the arc's two ends show
         a photograph rather than a rectangle of ground — the picture under
         this band got brighter at exactly that end, and tools/photo-meter.mjs
         caught the bridge line at 4.17 : 1 against 4.5. The open end never
         depended on the grade, so it is unchanged: 0.38 + 0.54 lands on the
         same 0.92 the old 0.26 + 0.66 did. Only the closed end deepens. */
      --k: calc(0.38 + 0.54 * var(--open));
      /* THE PLATEAU IS AS TALL AS THE BEAT STACK. The knee used to be at 24%
         of the band and the decay was well under way by 50% — which is where a
         two-line note at 390px actually sits. tools/photo-meter.mjs sampled
         that shoulder and read the bridge line at 4.17 : 1. The band is not
         heavier at its foot; the full weight simply reaches as far up the
         frame as the type does, and hands the picture back above it. */
      background: linear-gradient(0deg,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(var(--k) * 0.98)) 0%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / var(--k)) 34%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(var(--k) * 0.72)) 58%,
        rgb(3 9 16 / calc(var(--k) * 0.28)) 80%,
