    /* The eyebrow and the counter belong to the whole hold, so they arrive
       with the picture and leave with it, and never with a beat. They sit in
       the band the figure's own bar scrim guarantees. */
    /* THIS RAIL SITS CLEAR OF THE BAR'S REACH. Nav.astro paints a
       full-opacity band the height of the bar and a little past it, and it
       is opaque from the moment the page is scrolled at all. Anything set in
       the top of a full-bleed figure below the fold is therefore invisible —
       which is where Figure's own default `creditPos="top-*"` lands, and
       where this rail was. Both are moved clear of it here, together, so
       they stay on one line.

       The 5.5rem is NOT the tail's length and has not been since the tail
       came down to 3.5rem; it is a composed position that happens to have
       been derived from the old one. Do not read it as "the reach plus a
       gap" — the reach is `--nav-h + --nav-tail`, and this rail is set to
       clear it, not to coincide with it. STALE FIGURE CORRECTED TWICE — the
       wave-15 judge replaced 118px with 88.95px, and wave 16 shortened the
       tail again without touching this line. Measured off the live element
       (wave-16 judge, all three render modes, both viewports): --nav-tail is
       0.375rem, so the reach is 69.36px at 1440x900 (63.36 bar + 6 tail) and
       64px at 390x844. The 5.5rem below is therefore ~50px MORE clearance
       than the reach needs. That is the safe direction and nothing is broken
       by it, but it is no longer a composed position anyone can defend from
       the reach — if this rail is re-tuned, tune it as a composition and say
       so. Read it off the element before changing it; do not re-derive it
       from this comment.
       Nav's
       sync() now reads --nav-tail rather than retyping it, so the two
       cannot drift the way they did here. */
    :global(html.js) .held__top,
    :global(html.js) .held :global(.held__fig .fig__credit[data-pos^='top']) {
      top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 5.5rem + clamp(0.8rem, 2.4vh, 1.6rem));
    }
    /* It does NOT fade with the arc. A cream micro-label at 60% alpha over a
       sky that is still lit measures under 4.5:1 for the whole width of the
       fade, and there is no ink cheap enough to rescue it. The eyebrow names
       the scene for the whole hold, which is what an eyebrow is for.

       THE OLD CLAIM HERE — "at full weight, on the cap, it clears" — was
       true on the cap and false at the exit, and that is where it was wrong
       for four waves. On the cap this rail is at 166px and the bar's reach
       ends at 118, so it clears by 48px. But the stage is `position:
       sticky`: when the pin releases, the stage travels up and takes this
       rail through the bar with it, at full cream, with no opacity anywhere
       in its cascade. Measured on the glass, two renders per offset and
       scored row by row: 10.0 : 1 → 4.32 → 2.63 → 1.02, still fully painted,
       over about 36px of scroll. The coda link does the same thing 640px
       later, 15.7 : 1 → 1.03.

       (Both readings are subtraction, not a compositing model: the frame
       shot twice at one offset, with and without the string, so the ink is
       whatever light actually leaves the screen — scrim included. Every
       meter that models the ink and samples only the ground is blind here
       by construction, because the bar paints in FRONT of the glyphs.)

       It is fixed in Nav.astro, not here, and the reason is worth keeping:
       the failure is the bar's TAIL — a gradient that darkens the type and
       its ground together, which is why the curve ends at 1.0 and not at a
       floor. No ink under the type can lift it and no reposition can dodge
       it, because everything in a sticky stage crosses the top of the
       viewport on release. Nav now drops the tail for the length of this one
       scene and takes the hard edge it already had for the no-script case,
       so the rail is covered outright rather than half-lit. This rail keeps
       its position, its weight and its full-pin hold; nothing here moved. */
    :global(html.js) .held__top {
      position: absolute;
      left: 0; right: 0;
      z-index: 4;
      padding-block: 0;
    }
    :global(html.js) .held__meter { display: inline-flex; }

    /* Every beat is anchored to the SAME baseline, in one grid cell, so the
       type fades through in one place instead of hopping around the frame.
       A grid stack rather than absolute positioning, so the beats keep the
       shell's gutter and max-width like every other column on the site.

       ONE CELL WAS THE THING NOBODY HAD COSTED, so here is the costing. The
       seam between two beats carries no display line, and every cheap answer
       to that runs into this rule: two envelopes open in one cell superimpose
       two sentences' glyphs rather than crossfading two lines. So the
       question is whether the cell should be two.

       MEASURED on the homepage Forum scene, 1440x900. The beats are 133px and
       163px tall; the coda is 29px. Stacked in one cell the band is the
       TALLER of the two — 163px, from the 54px bottom pad to 217px up the
       frame, 24% of the viewport. In two cells it is their sum plus the row
       gap: 133 + 163 + 21.6 = 318px, reaching 372px, 41% of the viewport, a
       95% deeper band of type. (390x844: 83 + 114 + 20.3 = 217px against
       114px, on an 84.4px pad — 36% of the viewport against 24%.)

       That is not a layout cost, it is an INK cost, and it lands on the one
       thing this scene sells. `.held__ink` is 58% of the frame with its full
       weight held to 34% and handed back to the photograph above that — the
       plateau is exactly as tall as the beat stack, and the comment on that
       rule records what happened the last time type sat on its shoulder
       (tools/photo-meter.mjs, the bridge line at 4.17 : 1). A 372px stack
       tops out at 71% of a 522px band, where `--k` has already decayed past
       0.72 toward 0.28. Two cells therefore forces the ink taller and
       heavier over 41% of a frame whose argument is that the picture is
       handed back above the words — to buy a crossing where two half-alpha
       sentences are legible in two places at once, which is not "a
       proposition on screen" but two propositions half-erased.

       THE OTHER SHAPE, and it is worth recording because it nearly works. A
       WIPE-THROUGH keeps one cell and one baseline and superimposes nothing
       by construction: a moving vertical boundary, the arriving line drawn
       fully inked to its left, the departing line to its right, disjoint
       regions, fully-inked glyphs on the frame at every offset. It needs no
       new primitive — the clip-path vector is already here, on the note and
       the coda. It is dead on the copy, not on the mechanism: the two beats
       are "The Lion Forum" and "The people who build sit with the people who
       decide", so mid-crossing the baseline reads "The people who bui|Forum".
       A chimera, and a different one for every scene this component is ever
       given. A transition that is only safe for strings nobody has written
       yet is not a primitive.

       SO THE CELL STAYS ONE, and the seam's price is 337.7px per beat kept
       whole and a stack that never hops. What was actually available at the
       seam was not the type at all — it was the meter; see THE METER LATCHES
       in motion.js. */
    :global(html.js) .held__beats {
      position: absolute;
      left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
      z-index: 3;
      display: grid;
      align-items: end;
      row-gap: clamp(1.1rem, 2.4vh, 1.9rem);
      padding-block: 0 clamp(2.4rem, 6vh, 4.4rem);
    }
    :global(html.js) .held__beat {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
      align-self: end;
      /* the beat drifts UP continuously while its envelope carries it
         through: the text is what travels, and it never travels backwards */
      transform: translate3d(0, calc((0.42 - var(--bu, 0)) * 3.6vh), 0);
      will-change: transform;
    }
    /* ── LARGE TYPE FADES, SMALL TYPE WIPES ──────────────────────────
       Measured across the whole hold, not at rest: a 53px display line at
       half alpha over the beat ink still reads 7.5:1 against a 3:1 budget,
       while a 12px micro-label at the same half alpha reads 3.2:1 against a
       4.5:1 one. Small type has no room to spend on a crossfade. So the
       display line fades — that is the reference's grammar and the thing the
       reader is meant to feel — and everything in the micro registers is
       masked instead: drawn or not drawn, never half-inked. It is the site's
       existing `data-wipe` vector, driven by the same envelope. */
    :global(html.js) .held__line {
      opacity: var(--be, 1);
      will-change: opacity;
    }
    :global(html.js) .held__note,
    :global(html.js) .held__coda {
      clip-path: inset(0 calc((1 - var(--be, 1)) * 100%) 0 -0.2em);
    }
    :global(html.js) .held--right .held__note,
    :global(html.js) .held--right .held__coda {
      clip-path: inset(0 -0.2em 0 calc((1 - var(--be, 1)) * 100%));
    }
    :global(html.js) .held__coda { grid-row: 2; }
    :global(html.js) .held__line { max-width: 16ch; }
    :global(html.js) .held__beats.shell { padding-inline: var(--gutter); }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (max-width: 720px) {
